Problem Statement
Given the head of a sorted linked list, delete all duplicates such that each element appears only once.
Return the linked list sorted as well.
Input: 1 -> 1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 3
Output: 1 -> 2 -> 3

Approach:
Since the linked list is sorted, duplicates appear adjacent.
Traverse the list and compare current node with next node.
If values are equal, skip the duplicate node by updating pointer.
Else, move forward.

///////////CODE/////////////


class Solution {
    public ListNode deleteDuplicates(ListNode head) {
        ListNode temp=head;
        while(temp !=null && temp.next!=null){
            if(temp.val==temp.next.val){
                temp.next=temp.next.next;
            }else{
                temp=temp.next;
            }
        }
        return head;

        
    }
}


--------TIME AND SPACE COMPLEXITY---------
Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

Reason:
We traverse the list once and modify pointers in place.
No extra data structure is used.

-------EDGE CASES--------
Edge Cases:
- Empty list (head == null)
- Single node list
- All elements same
- No duplicates


-------KEY LEARNINGS--------
- Proper null checking to avoid NullPointerException
- Importance of while (node != null && node.next != null)
- In-place pointer manipulation
  
while (node != null && node.next != null)
It means that Keep running the loop only if:
1.	Current node exists
2.	Next node also exists"
________________________________________
Why Do We Need Both Conditions?
Because in many linked list problems, we compare:
node.val with node.next.val
If node.next is null and you try: node.next.val
Java throws: NullPointerException


 
